From e4270b7f3b3a66354df0159dd90794c514c12937 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Pfister <119864532+JJRPF@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:25:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/37] arm64: dts: apple: t602x: add an energy model for the
 CPUs

The T602x CPU OPP tables have no opp-microwatt, so no energy model is
registered for the CPUs and the scheduler's energy-aware placement
(EAS) is off on M2 Pro/Max: /sys/kernel/debug/energy_model is empty on a
J414s although sched_energy_aware is 1 and every policy runs schedutil.
T8103, T600x, T8112 and T8140 have measured values.

T8112 (M2) has the same Blizzard and Avalanche cores. Its measured
per-OPP power is fitted to P = (a + b f) V^2 f per core type, with V the
J414s's own voltage for each OPP (ADT /arm-io/pmgr voltage-states1 and
voltage-states5), and the fit gives the T602x values: within 6.3% rms of
the T8112 measurements for both core types. The energy per cycle rises
from each OPP to the next, so no OPP is marked inefficient and cpufreq
keeps choosing among all frequencies as before; only task placement
changes. tools/aurora-sep/t6020-cpu-em.py derives the values and writes
them; its tests check that the tree carries them.

These are derived, not measured on T602x: measuring them needs a quiet
machine and the SMC's cluster power meter, as was done for T8112.

